index 0000000..e0f50bd
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/PORT.md
@@ -0,0 +1,288 @@
+# Porting PR #78 (the spill tier) onto v1.8 as patch `0084`
+
+Upstream [pull request #78](https://github.com/MiaAI-Lab/GLM-5.3-Flash-EXL3-2x-DGX-Sparks-TensorFold/pull/78) by
+[wojo](https://github.com/wojo) adds the spill tier as patch `0078`, against main at **v1.7.1** (76 patches). This
+recipe is at **v1.8** (82 patches), where `0078` is already taken by `0078-glm-take-over-decide-then-copy` and five
+more patches landed (`0079`-`0083`). This note records how the port was made, what was decided where, what was
+checked, and what the maintainers still have to decide.
+
+Scope of the port: the pull request's own changes only. No TensorFold or torch upgrade, no new subsystem, and the
+tier stays **off by default** (`SPILL_GIB=0`: no flag passed, no directory mounted, the engine paths skipped).
+
+- Patch: `patches/0084-glm-spill-tier.patch` (renumbered; `0078` is taken).
+- Recipe side: `scripts/config.sh`, `start.sh`, `tools/pool_room_check.py`, `tools/test_queued_cancellation.py`,
+  `README.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`, `CREDITS.md`, `NOTICE`.
+- New: `tools/spill_tier_check.py` and `tools/spill_scripts_check.sh` (CPU; the pull request ships no
+  test file).
+- `patches/0001`-`0083` are **byte-identical** to v1.8's, `0078` and the `#48`/`#63`/`#64` patches included. Nothing
+  was rebased, re-sorted or re-hunked.
+
+## How the patch was made: a three-way merge per file, not a re-apply
+
+The pull request's patch was **not** applied to the v1.8 tree and it was **not** stacked on `0078`. Four trees were
+reconstructed from pristine TensorFold v0.6.0 (`c4646171139ee8a3c38103eaa1699dad226ec12b`, `src/tensorfold`) by
+`patch -p0 --forward`, and the merge ran per file with `git merge-file --diff3`:
+
+| role | tree | contents |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| base | `tf-v171` | v0.6.0 + `0001`-`0075`, `0077` (main at v1.7.1 -- the pull request's own base) |
+| ours | `tf-v18` | v0.6.0 + `0001`-`0083` (this recipe, unchanged) |
+| theirs | `tf-v171-spill` | `tf-v171` + the pull request's patch, exactly as published |
+
+The merged tree is then diffed against **unchanged** `tf-v18` to produce `0084`. So `0084` is by construction the
+difference the tier makes *on top of v1.8*, and every v1.8 feature in a file the tier also touches is kept by the
+merge rather than by hand.
+
+Nine files change (the pull request's nine). Of those, v1.8 had touched three; the other six v1.8 never touched, so
+"theirs" was taken whole.
+
+| file | v1.8 side | PR side | resolution |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `cli.py` | -- | `SpillConfig.from_args`, `app.on_exit`, `_flush_on_exit` | theirs |
+| `cli_args.py` | -- | `--spill-highwater/-min-tokens/-min-free-gib/-keep-builds`, `--spill-gib` help | theirs |
+| `cuda/health.py` | -- | `/health`'s `spill` section | theirs |
+| `cuda/spill.py` | -- | new, ~1,230 lines | theirs (new file) |
+| `families/glm5_next/__init__.py` | -- | `CUDA_SPILL`, `spill=` option | theirs |
+| `server/metrics.py` | -- | `tensorfold_health:spill_*` | theirs |
+| `cuda/http.py` | `0081` TFCAP admission headers in `_json`/`prepare` | shutdown hook on `KeyboardInterrupt` | auto-merge, **disjoint** |
+| `families/glm5_next/cuda/multi.py` | `0082` `max_in_system`, `_check_admission` in `submit` | ~530 lines: pool hook, LOAD/SPILL/FLUSH, guarded pool moves | auto-merge, **disjoint** (see below) |
+| `families/glm5_next/cuda/engine.py` | `0078` rewrote `_take_over` | ~220 lines, incl. two new steps in `_take_over` | **one conflict, resolved by hand** (below) |
+
+`git merge-file` reported exactly **one** conflict, in `_take_over`. A clean auto-merge is not by itself proof of a
+correct merge, so each auto-merged region was read against both sides; `cuda/http.py` and `multi.py` touch
+genuinely different parts of their files and are recorded as disjoint above. The merged `engine.py` differs from
+"theirs" **only** inside `_take_over`, and the merged `multi.py` differs from "theirs" only in `0082`'s two places
+(the `CapacityError` import, and `max_in_system` + `_check_admission` in `GlmScheduler`) -- both verified by diffing
+the merged file against `tf-v171-spill`.
+
+## The one conflict: `_take_over` (the critical hunk)
+
+Both sides rewrite the tail of the single-stream `GlmEngine._take_over`, for different reasons.
+
+- **`0078` (ours)** turned one interleaved walk into *decide first, copy after*: the walk only accounts sizes
+  (`held()`, `forget()`), then every drop runs, then only the survivors are cloned. Cloning each in turn and dropping
+  it for the next grew torch's pool with every larger copy (reserved memory far past the budget; the driver logged
+  `NV_ERR_NO_MEMORY`). It also replaced the old `dropped` flag with `over_budget`, so `empty_cache()` fires for a
+  drop made *for the budget*, not for a snapshot whose rows were already gone.
+- **The pull request (theirs)** added two steps to the old single walk: after each `save_rows`, a state whose DFlash2
+  window `save_rows` just dropped is written to disk *from the saved rows*, with the window it had restored around
+  the call; and after the walk, `_spill_wait()` so a pending copy off the device ends before the next prefill
+  overwrites the live caches.
+
+Both were kept. The merged shape (and why each piece sits where it does):
+
+```
+  walk over `order`            # 0078: accounting only -- forget(), held(), over_budget
+  for snap in drops:           # 0078: every drop before any copy
+      self._drop(snap)         #   (each _drop may queue a spill job from views of the live caches)
+  for snap in order:           # 0078: only the survivors are cloned
+      if id(snap) in saved:
+          window, end = snap.drafter_rows, snap.drafter_end
+          save_rows(...)       #   reads the live caches; it does not change them
+          if spill and end != snap.drafter_end:
+              ... self._spill_snap(snap) ...   # PR: beside the save_rows it reads from, window restored
+  if spill: self._spill_wait() # PR: every pending copy leaves the device ...
+  if over_budget:              # 0078: ... before the pool is given the rows back,
+      torch.cuda.empty_cache() #        and before the prefill that follows
+```
+
+Three ordering decisions, and the reasoning behind each:
+
+1. **The per-snapshot write moved into the second loop.** It reads the rows `save_rows` just saved, so it has to
+   follow that snapshot's own `save_rows` -- which under `0078` happens in the second loop, not the first.
+2. **`_spill_wait()` after both loops, before `empty_cache()`.** The drops are what *queue* the jobs, so waiting
+   before them would be pointless; `save_rows` only reads the live caches, so it is safe to run with jobs pending.
+   The hazards are `empty_cache()` returning rows to torch's pool and the prefill that follows overwriting the live
+   caches -- both after the second loop. This is the "guarded pool move".
+3. **`empty_cache()` keeps `over_budget`, not `dropped`.** That is `0078`'s fix, and the tier gives no reason to undo
+   it: a snapshot dropped because its rows were already gone frees nothing new.
+
+**One behavioural difference from "theirs", deliberate and benign.** `_drop` consults `_superseded`, which reads
+`self.cache`. Under `0078` all drops happen before any `save_rows`, so a `_spill_snap(leaving=True)` during the drop
+loop sees a cache that is already fully pruned, where upstream's interleaved walk saw it partly pruned. The effect is
+confined to eviction bookkeeping: a short state can now be judged *superseded* by a longer one that is about to be
+written in the second loop (and is), where upstream would have written both. Fewer redundant files and the same
+restores; `unwritten_superseded` counts one more. `self.live` is unchanged at that point either way, so which states
+are writable at all does not change.
+
+## What the port had to keep right, and where it is kept
+
+- **`0072`'s display span (`DISPLAY_KV_MIB`), both directions.** The spanned latent planes sit over two
+  `cuMemHostRegister` registrations and CUDA refuses a memcpy across them. The pull request already handles this (it
+  was one of its three fits to v1.7): `multi.py` passes `spanned=[f"p{i}" ...  if p.spanned]` to the store, and
+  `spill.py`'s `_by_kernel` moves those rows through an 8 MiB staging buffer with the same `bitwise_or` kernel
+  `0072` uses -- a kernel *into* the buffer then a memcpy out when writing, and a memcpy in then a kernel *out* when
+  reading. Both are "theirs" code, taken verbatim; the `spanned=` registration is checked to survive the merge.
+  Note `display` is only non-zero with `--parallel 2` or more (and then `cache_bytes = 0`: kept prompts live in the
+  pool), so the single-stream engine's own store, which passes no `spanned=`, cannot meet a spanned plane.
+- **Guarded pool moves.** `multi.py`'s `_guard_pool` wraps `pool.remove`/`move`/`resize` with `_release_rows`, and
+  `_rewrites` covers a take-over in place. `0074` compacts through `pool.move`, so its moves are guarded too.
+  Unchanged by the merge (v1.8 does not touch that part of `multi.py`).
+- **`0082`'s admission cap.** Kept: the `CapacityError` import, `max_in_system=max_streams + TF_GLM_MAX_QUEUED`, and
+  `self._check_admission(background)` first thing in `GlmScheduler.submit`. The tier's own scheduler state
+  (`flushing`, `flush_lock`, `loading`, `ready`, `staged`, `_Flush`) initialises beside it. `_Flush` carries
+  `background = False` and `emit = None`, so `Waiting.put`, `Waiting.foreground_count` and `0073`'s `_cancel_item`
+  all handle it without a special case.
+- **`0073`'s queued cancellation.** `_cancel_waiting` now also reaps `ready` and `loading`; `_cancel_item` drops the
+  id from `staged`.
+- **Ranks fail closed.** `SpillStore.__init__` gathers a flag from every rank and raises if *any* rank cannot use its
+  directory; `save` agrees the size and the free-disk floor in one all-gather and writes on no rank if one rank is
+  under the floor; a rank running another build or settings is named at start.
