+## Review round: two script-integration blockers, fixed
+
+The runtime port passed review; two **script** integration blockers did not, and are fixed here. The patch itself
+(`patches/0084-glm-spill-tier.patch`) is **unchanged** by these fixes -- same 130,344 bytes, same tree hash. Only
+`start.sh`, `stop.sh` and `scripts/config.sh` changed.
+
+**1. The mount was keyed on raw `SPILL_GIB`, not on the effective flag.** `SERVE_ARGS` appends `"$@"` *after* the
+config-derived flags, so an operator's command line wins for the server but the old
+`if [[ "$SPILL_GIB" != 0 ]]` mount block never saw it. `SPILL_GIB=0 ./start.sh --spill-gib 64` therefore started a
+server with the tier on, **nothing bound at `/spill`** and no `TF_SPILL_FLUSH_S`: the tier would have written into
+the container's own layer -- invisible while it ran and gone at the next `docker rm`. The mirror case
+(`SPILL_GIB=64 ./start.sh --spill-gib 0`) mounted a directory nothing used.
+
+Both now go through the recipe's existing `arg_value`, which already resolves a flag's last occurrence in either
+`--flag value` or `--flag=value` form. The gate sits immediately after `arg_value` is defined -- *before* start.sh
+stops or starts anything -- and sets `SPILL_ON` / `SPILL_MOUNT`; the `RUN_ARGS` block keys on `SPILL_ON` and binds
+the host `SPILL_DIR` to the **effective** `--snapshot-dir`, so an absolute override is honoured rather than ignored.
+What cannot be supported is refused there, with the reason, before any container is touched: `--snapshot-dir none`
+(in memory only, so it cannot hold the tier), a relative `--snapshot-dir`, `--spill-gib` with no `--snapshot-dir` at
+all, and a relative `SPILL_DIR`. No silent container-layer state in any path.
+
+**2. The spill `mkdir` ran under `DRY_RUN=1`.** It created `SPILL_DIR` here *and* ran `mkdir -p` on every worker over
+ssh, while `DRY_RUN=1` promises to change nothing anywhere. It now prints the plan
+(`DRY_RUN: would create ... and mount it at ...`) and does neither; the `-v`/`-e` arguments are still assembled, so
+the dry run shows the mount it would use. Deliberately narrow: the pre-existing dry-run behaviour of
+`KERNEL_CACHE` and the other state directories is **not** touched.
+
+**3. Related lifecycle fix (same root cause).** `scripts/config.sh` raises `STOP_TIMEOUT` to `SPILL_FLUSH_S + 30`
+only when it sees `SPILL_GIB` set, so an ephemeral `SPILL_GIB=64 ./start.sh` followed by a plain `./stop.sh` cut the
+60 s shutdown flush off at 30 s. `scripts/config.sh` now has `spill_stop_timeout()`, which reads the effective
+`--spill-gib` out of the **container that is actually running** (`docker inspect` of `.Config.Cmd`, both flag forms)
+and its `TF_SPILL_FLUSH_S` (`.Config.Env`, 60 assumed if absent), and returns `flush + 30`. It is read-only, it only
+ever **raises** the value, an explicit `STOP_TIMEOUT` (environment, `scripts/local.sh`, `.env` -- captured as
+`STOP_TIMEOUT_EXPLICIT` before config.sh computes its own) always wins, and with no container here the configured
+value stands. `stop.sh` computes it once and uses it at all six of its timeout sites, dry-run messages included;
+start.sh's own two stop sites use it too, since a restart faces the same hazard. No new persistent state, no
+manager, no watchdog.
+
+On head-plus-workers: the existing pattern is enough. `stop_worker` passes the same inferred number into the
+worker's `docker stop -t`, and every rank runs the same `Cmd`, so rank 0's container answers for all of them; rank 1
+has no SIGTERM handler of its own by design (it follows rank 0's FLUSH and exits when rank 0's store closes), so it
+needs the same budget, not a separate inference. No ssh is added to the stop path.
+
