+## Known limits of these checks
+
+- **Everything here is CPU.** No GPU, server, inference or cluster check was run, by instruction. The pull request's
+  own GPU evidence (TTFT, decode, exactness against a fresh `draft: false` prefill, fault injection, `kill -9`) is
+  upstream's and was **not** reproduced; the README table is labelled as upstream's v1.5/v1.7.1 numbers.
+- **The spanned check cannot tell the two paths apart on a CPU**, where both are memory copies. It pins the
+  registration (`spanned=` reaching the store, which is what the merge could have dropped) and that the kernel path
+  writes the same bytes and header as the memcpy path over more than one CRC block. The actual CUDA refusal needs the
+  GPU check (`tools/display_kv_check.py --gpu`) on a Spark with `DISPLAY_KV_MIB` on.
+- **`tools/spill_tier_check.py` uses a stand-in class, not `decode.Snapshot`**, so it covers the store and the
+  encode/decode contract, not GLM's own fields.
+- `0084` was not re-measured for the image hash; `IMAGE_TAG`/`IMAGE_DIGEST` are untouched, so `scripts/prepare.sh`
+  will build locally until a release publishes the new tag.
